"Frames a product challenge as a business problem and links outcomes, user needs, solution ideas and testable hypotheses.\n\nUse when: Align a team before exploratory product work. Choose small experiments that address the riskiest parts of an idea.\n\nAvoid when: The team has already committed to a solution and cannot reconsider its assumptions.\n\nVisual grammar: Labeled regions distinguish the business problem, outcomes, users and benefits. Solution ideas remain separate from hypotheses and experiments. Short links express the proposed relationship between user and business outcomes.\n\nBuild: State the business problem and observable outcome changes. Identify relevant users and the benefits that could change their behavior. Form hypotheses and select the smallest useful experiment for the riskiest claim.\n\nCheck: Outcomes describe changed behavior or results rather than shipped features. User benefit is explicit rather than assumed. Experiments produce evidence relevant to a named hypothesis.\n\nPitfalls: Turning the canvas into a project charter for a predetermined feature. Calling delivery milestones learning outcomes.\n\nExample: A class provider connects missed bookings to learner scheduling uncertainty, explores flexible reminders and tests whether those reminders change reservation behavior.\n\nAlternatives: Test card, Business Model Canvas."
